    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "### Should you make something of the all-women panel?\n\n**In short: No.** You should not raise or make an issue of the panel being composed entirely of women. Doing so is unlikely to help your case and would almost certainly backfire professionally.\n\nHere is why:\n\n1. **No indication of gender bias:** Both you and the successful candidate are women. An all-female panel selecting a female candidate provides no basis for a claim of gender discrimination. \n2. **Operational reality in public broadcasting:** Public broadcasters often have policies requiring panels to be balanced (frequently designed to avoid all-male panels). Depending on department leadership (e.g., Head of News, Senior HR partner, Digital Managing Editor, external assessor), it is increasingly common for the relevant senior stakeholders to happen to be women. \n3. **Professional risk:** Questioning the panel’s composition when you were not selected—particularly when the successful candidate is of the same gender—risks being perceived as grasping for grievances rather than accepting the competitive nature of the process.\n\n---\n\n### What the decision is more likely a sign of\n\nRather than the gender composition of the panel, the details you shared point toward standard—and very common—dynamics in modern newsrooms:\n\n* **The digital pivot:** Public broadcasters worldwide are under intense pressure to transform from legacy broadcast models to digital-first, multiplatform newsrooms (apps, streaming, social video, and mobile-first formats). A candidate with 8 years of experience and deep digital fluency may align more closely with the station’s strategic roadmap than a candidate with 15 years whose background may be viewed (fairly or unfairly) as primarily traditional broadcast.\n* **Years of experience vs. strategic fit:** While having 15 years of experience (including 5 as deputy editor) makes you exceptionally qualified on paper, final interviews typically shift away from CV length to **vision, interview execution, and cultural fit**. At the final stage of three candidates across two interviews, margins are usually razor-thin.\n* **Potential age dynamics:** While ageism can exist in newsroom transitions (especially where \"digital\" is used as a proxy for younger leadership), the gender of the panel does not make this more or less likely. However, without clear evidence, alleging age bias based purely on the successful candidate's digital background is difficult to substantiate.\n\n---\n\n### What to do about the delay in feedback\n\nWaiting two weeks for written feedback from a public broadcaster is frustrating, but it is **not unusual**. Public sector and public media HR processes move slowly: formal feedback often requires written sign-offs from both HR and the hiring manager to ensure consistency and compliance.\n\n#### Recommended Next Steps:\n\n1. **Send a polite, professional follow-up:**\n   Frame your request around personal and professional development rather than challenging the outcome:\n   > *\"Dear [HR Contact / Panel Chair],*  \n   > *I hope you are well. I’m writing to follow up on my request from [date] regarding written feedback on my recent interview for the News Editor position. As I remain very committed to my professional development and the broadcaster’s mission, I would greatly appreciate any insights the panel could share regarding areas where I might strengthen my profile for future senior opportunities. Thank you again for your time and guidance.\"*\n\n2. **Focus your questions on the digital gap:**\n   When you do receive feedback (or if you are offered a phone call), focus on specifics:\n   * Ask what specific digital or strategic competencies the panel felt tipped the balance.\n   * Inquire about the broadcaster’s future editorial priorities so you know how to position yourself next time.\n\nBy keeping your focus strictly on constructive feedback and strategic alignment, you maintain your standing as a strong 